# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = '1 Samuel'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'Samuel, the Demand for a King, Saul’s Rejection, and David Anointed'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '1 Samuel moves from Hannah’s prayer and the birth of Samuel to the people’s demand for a king. It records the anointing of Saul, his rejection for disobedience, the anointing of David, David’s victory over Goliath, and David’s flight from Saul (1 Sam. 1:20; 8:5; 10:1; 13:14; 15:23; 16:13; 17:45).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD is Israel’s King and the monarchy is set up within Israel’s covenant life in the land (1 Sam. 8:7; 12:22).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 1 Samuel', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Hannah and Samuel (Chs. 1–3)'), ('p', 'Hannah vows and bears Samuel, her song declares that none is holy as the LORD, and the LORD calls the child Samuel (1 Sam. 1:11, 20; 2:2; 3:1, 10, 19).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Demand for a King (Chs. 4–8)'), ('p', 'Israel asks, “make us a king to judge us like all the nations,” and the LORD says they have rejected Him (1 Sam. 8:5, 7).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Saul Anointed and Rejected (Chs. 9–15)'), ('p', 'Samuel anoints Saul, Saul is chosen, and the LORD later rejects him because he did not keep His commandment (1 Sam. 9:16; 10:1, 24; 13:14; 15:22–23).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — David (Chs. 16–31)'), ('p', 'Samuel anoints David, the Spirit of the LORD comes upon him, David defeats Goliath, and he spares Saul as the LORD’s anointed (1 Sam. 16:13; 17:45, 47; 24:6).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 1 Samuel', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The LORD Is Unlike Any</b> — “There is none holy as the LORD: for there is none beside thee: neither is there any rock like our God” (1 Sam. 2:2).', '<b>A King Demanded</b> — “Behold, thou art old, and thy sons walk not in thy ways: now make us a king to judge us like all the nations” (1 Sam. 8:5).', '<b>The LORD Rejected as King</b> — “They have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me, that I should not reign over them” (1 Sam. 8:7).', '<b>Obedience Over Sacrifice</b> — “To obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams” (1 Sam. 15:22).', '<b>The LORD’s Anointed</b> — “The LORD forbid that I should do this thing unto my master, the LORD’s anointed” (1 Sam. 24:6).', '<b>David Anointed</b> — “The Spirit of the LORD came upon David from that day forward” (1 Sam. 16:13).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 1 Samuel', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how 1 Samuel points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Anointed One</b> — Saul and David are each called the LORD’s anointed (1 Sam. 10:1; 24:6), and the word points forward in Israel’s story to the King who is to come.', '<b>The Man After His Own Heart</b> — The LORD “hath sought him a man after his own heart” (1 Sam. 13:14); the line of David leads to the promised King.', '<b>The Battle Is the LORD’s</b> — “The battle is the LORD’s” (1 Sam. 17:47) is spoken by David to Goliath’s side.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in 1 Samuel', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 1 Samuel speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. 1 Samuel operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 1 Samuel in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', '1 Samuel reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['covenant life in the land under the LORD as King (1 Sam. 8:7)', 'the monarchy, first Saul and then David (1 Sam. 10:1; 16:13)', 'the anointed king (1 Sam. 24:6)', 'the promise that the LORD will not forsake His people (1 Sam. 12:22)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 1 Samuel that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 1 Samuel contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Samuel Asked of the LORD — “Because I have asked him of the LORD.” (1 Sam. 1:20).', 'Israel Asks for a King — “now make us a king to judge us like all the nations.” (1 Sam. 8:5).', 'Saul Anointed — “Is it not because the LORD hath anointed thee to be captain over his inheritance?” (1 Sam. 10:1).', 'The LORD Will Not Forsake His People — “the LORD will not forsake his people for his great name’s sake.” (1 Sam. 12:22).', 'A Man After His Own Heart — “the LORD hath sought him a man after his own heart.” (1 Sam. 13:14).', 'David Anointed — “Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren.” (1 Sam. 16:13).', 'The LORD’s Anointed — “seeing he is the anointed of the LORD.” (1 Sam. 24:6).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 1 Samuel Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 1 Samuel and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 1 Samuel teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 1 Samuel', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Samuel Asked of the LORD', '“Because I have asked him of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 1:20'], ['Israel Asks for a King', '“now make us a king to judge us like all the nations.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 8:5'], ['Saul Anointed', '“Is it not because the LORD hath anointed thee to be captain over his inheritance?”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 10:1'], ['The LORD Will Not Forsake His People', '“the LORD will not forsake his people for his great name’s sake.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 12:22'], ['A Man After His Own Heart', '“the LORD hath sought him a man after his own heart.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 13:14'], ['David Anointed', '“Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 16:13'], ['The LORD’s Anointed', '“seeing he is the anointed of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Sam. 24:6']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 1 Samuel that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 1 Samuel teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The LORD Is Holy', '“There is none holy as the LORD.”', '1 Sam. 2:2'], ['Life and Death Are His', '“The LORD killeth, and maketh alive.”', '1 Sam. 2:6'], ['Obedience Over Offering', '“to obey is better than sacrifice.”', '1 Sam. 15:22'], ['The Battle Is the LORD’s', '“the LORD saveth not with sword and spear: for the battle is the LORD’s.”', '1 Sam. 17:47']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 1 Samuel without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to 1 Samuel in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Honour the holiness of God</b> — None is holy as the LORD (1 Sam. 2:2).', '<b>Remember that life is in His hand</b> — He killeth and maketh alive (1 Sam. 2:6).', '<b>Choose obedience</b> — It is better than sacrifice (1 Sam. 15:22).', '<b>Trust Him in the fight</b> — The battle is the LORD’s (1 Sam. 17:47).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 1 Samuel', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 1 Samuel in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Hannah and Samuel (Chs. 1–3) — The child who hears. <i>(1 Sam. 1:20; 2:2; 3:10)</i>', 'The Demand for a King (Chs. 4–8) — Israel rejects the LORD as King. <i>(1 Sam. 8:5, 7)</i>', 'Saul (Chs. 9–15) — Anointed and rejected. <i>(1 Sam. 10:1; 13:14; 15:22–23)</i>', 'David (Chs. 16–31) — Anointed and tested. <i>(1 Sam. 16:13; 17:45, 47; 24:6)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 1 Samuel, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward trust, obedience, and humility without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 1 Samuel simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, 1 Samuel stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
